+++
title = "Installation"
template = "docs"
+++

Revela runs on **Windows**, **macOS**, and **Linux**. It comes in three editions. If you just want to publish your photos, pick **Standalone**.

| | **Standalone** (recommended) | **Full** | **.NET Global Tool** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Prerequisites | None | None | .NET 10 SDK |
| Plugins & themes | All built in | Installed from the bundled `packages/` folder | Installed from release `.nupkg` files |
| Plugin/theme management | Not needed (not available) | `revela plugin …`, `revela theme install …`, `revela restore` | Same as Full |
| Custom plugins | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |

The [Downloads](../../../pages/downloads/) page has direct links for every edition.

---

## Standalone (Recommended)

A single executable with the Lumina theme, its Statistics and Calendar extensions, and all six official plugins (Compress, Serve, Statistics, Calendar, Source.OneDrive, Source.Calendar) built in. There is nothing to install afterwards and no internet connection is needed to build your site.

The easiest way is the [Downloads](../../../pages/downloads/) page, or the newest release on [GitHub Releases](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases). Extract the archive into a folder of your choice.

> **Beta note:** every Revela release is currently a pre-release, so GitHub's `/releases/latest/` links don't work yet. The commands below look up the newest release first.

### Windows (x64)

In PowerShell:

```powershell
$tag = (Invoke-RestMethod https://api.github.com/repos/Spectara/Revela/releases)[0].tag_name
Invoke-WebRequest "https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases/download/$tag/revela-win-x64-standalone.zip" -OutFile revela.zip
Expand-Archive revela.zip -DestinationPath D:\Revela
```

### macOS (Apple Silicon)

```bash
TAG=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/Spectara/Revela/releases | grep -m1 '"tag_name"' | cut -d '"' -f4)
curl -L "https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases/download/$TAG/revela-osx-arm64-standalone.tar.gz" | tar xz
chmod +x revela
```

### Linux (x64)

```bash
TAG=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/Spectara/Revela/releases | grep -m1 '"tag_name"' | cut -d '"' -f4)
curl -L "https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases/download/$TAG/revela-linux-x64-standalone.tar.gz" | tar xz
chmod +x revela
```

Keep the native library that comes in the archive (`libvips-42.dll`, `libvips.42.dylib` or `libvips.so.42`) next to the executable. Builds for Windows ARM64 and Linux ARM64 are also on the [releases page](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases).

> **Tip:** Add the folder that contains `revela` to your `PATH`, so you can run `revela` from any project folder.

---

## Full (Modular)

The `revela` executable plus a `packages/` folder with every official plugin and theme as a `.nupkg` file. Revela uses that folder as an offline package feed named `bundled`, so you choose which plugins and themes to install, and you can add third-party or your own plugins.

Download `revela-<platform>-full.zip` (Windows) or `revela-<platform>-full.tar.gz` (macOS/Linux) from the [releases page](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases) and extract it. Keep the `packages/` folder next to the executable.

On first run, Revela starts the **Setup Wizard**, which installs themes and plugins from `packages/`. Choose **Full Installation** to install everything, or **Custom Installation** to pick packages. Revela exits after installing; start it again to continue. You can install or remove packages later:

```bash
revela plugin install Statistics
revela theme install Lumina.Statistics
revela plugin list
```

See [Plugins](../../plugins/) for details.

---

## .NET Global Tool

If you have the [.NET SDK](https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download) (version 10 or later), you can install Revela as a global tool.

> **Not on NuGet.org yet:** Revela packages are not published on NuGet.org, so `dotnet tool install -g Spectara.Revela` on its own will fail. Every package is attached to each [GitHub Release](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases) instead.

1. Download `Spectara.Revela.<version>.nupkg` from the release into a folder, for example `./revela-packages`. Download the plugin and theme `.nupkg` files you want into the same folder (at least `Spectara.Revela.Themes.Lumina.<version>.nupkg`).
2. Install the tool from that folder:

   ```bash
   dotnet tool install -g Spectara.Revela --version <version> --add-source ./revela-packages
   ```

3. Tell Revela where to find plugins and themes (the tool has no bundled packages):

   ```bash
   revela config feed add releases /absolute/path/to/revela-packages
   ```

   This adds the folder to `dependencies.feeds` in your global `revela.json`, so it is trusted for every project.

The tool has the same commands as the Full edition.

---

## First Run

Revela always works on the **current folder**: the folder you start it in is your project. To create a site:

1. Create an empty folder for your site, for example `D:\Photos\MyPortfolio`.
2. Open a terminal in that folder and run `revela`. (If you double-click the executable or the `Start Revela` launcher instead, the folder that contains Revela becomes the project.)
3. Revela finds no `project.json` and offers **Create New Project**. The project wizard asks for:
   1. Project settings (name, base URL)
   2. Directory paths (`source` and `output` by default)
   3. Theme (Lumina)
   4. Image settings (formats and quality)
   5. Site metadata (title, author, …)

   It then offers optional steps, such as connecting a OneDrive folder.
4. Put your photos in `source/` and choose **generate → all**.

The wizard creates `project.json` and `site.json` in the folder. The [User Journey](../user-journey/) walks through the whole workflow.

> **Full edition and .NET tool:** on the very first start, the Setup Wizard installs themes and plugins before the project wizard runs.

---

## Verifying Installation

```bash
revela --version
```

For more detail (plugin and theme counts, active theme):

```bash
revela info
```

The version line ends with the edition, for example `— Standalone edition` or `— Full edition`.

Check where Revela stores its configuration:

```bash
revela config locations
```

The global `revela.json` lives next to the executable when that folder is writable (for example an extracted ZIP). Otherwise it goes to `%APPDATA%\Revela\` on Windows or `~/.config/Revela/` on macOS and Linux.

---

## Updating

### Standalone

Download the latest release and replace the executable (and the native `libvips` library next to it). Your projects are ordinary folders and are not touched. Themes and plugins are built in, so they update with the executable.

### Full

Extract the new release over the old one, including the new `packages/` folder. Then run this in each project to install the matching versions:

```bash
revela restore
```

### .NET Global Tool

Download the new release's `.nupkg` files into your package folder, then:

```bash
dotnet tool update -g Spectara.Revela --version <version> --add-source ./revela-packages
revela restore
```

---

## Troubleshooting

### `revela: command not found`

Revela isn't on your `PATH`.

- **Windows**: add the folder containing `revela.exe` to your `Path` environment variable.
- **macOS / Linux**: create a symlink (`sudo ln -s /path/to/revela /usr/local/bin/revela`) or add the folder to your `PATH`.

### `Permission denied` (macOS / Linux)

The binary isn't executable yet:

```bash
chmod +x /path/to/revela
```

### Windows SmartScreen warning

The executable isn't code-signed yet. Click **More info → Run anyway**.

### macOS "cannot be opened because the developer cannot be verified"

Allow it once under **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Open Anyway**, or clear the quarantine flag from the terminal:

```bash
xattr -d com.apple.quarantine /path/to/revela
```

### Something else looks wrong

Run this inside your project folder:

```bash
revela check
```

It checks configuration, folder structure, theme and content, and tells you how to fix each problem.
